  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#0",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides",
    "text": "Loomail is unconventional in one deliberate way: there is no visual flow builder. Flows are written as plain-language prompts, compiled once into a deterministic plan, and executed by an engine that never calls an AI. This page explains that model, then gives short guides for the tasks people actually try first.\n\nContents:\n\n- The mental model\n- How an event becomes an email\n- Compilation: prompts in, deterministic plans out\n- The approval queue\n- Three ways to produce email content\n- Flow mechanics (triggers, classes, re-entry, windows)\n- Guides\n\n---"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#1",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > The mental model",
    "text": "Four objects, one direction:\n\n```\nEvent  ->  Contact  ->  Flow membership  ->  Message\n(track/identify)  (one per userId)  (compiled plan executes)  (one email)\n```\n\n- **Events** arrive through the ingestion API (`/v1/track`, `/v1/identify`, `/v1/batch`, Segment-compatible). See INGESTION.md.\n- **Contacts** are the people. One contact per `userId` (stored as `external_id`). Email addresses are optional and unique when present. Every contact has a lifecycle state (`signed_up`, `activated`, `engaged`, `at_risk`, `dormant`, `churned`, `resurrected`) that the engine maintains from their activity.\n- **Flows** are the automations. A flow has a trigger, a compiled plan of steps, and policies. When a contact matches a trigger, the engine enrolls them: they become a flow membership.\n- **Messages** are individual emails. Each step a membership reaches creates exactly one message, which is generated, optionally approved, and sent.\n\nThe engine is deterministic. Everything below the flow is Postgres jobs and SQL. AI participates in exactly two places, both off the execution path: compiling a flow's prompt into its plan, and drafting a message's copy before it enters the approval queue."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#2",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > How an event becomes an email",
    "text": "Using the quickstart path as the example (`signed_up` event, welcome flow active):"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#3",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > How an event becomes an email",
    "text": "1. `POST /v1/track` stores the event and resolves the contact (find-or-create by `userId`). Identifying first with `traits.email` gives the contact an address; without one, messages cannot be sent to them.\n2. Ingestion immediately queues a trigger check. Active, compiled flows whose trigger matches the event enroll the contact. Guards run here: suppression list first, then the one-active-nurture-flow rule, then the re-entry policy.\n3. The membership advances to step 1. Its delay (`0m` for the welcome email) has already elapsed, so a message is created.\n4. Content generation produces the copy. For the library flow this is instant (fixed templates); for AI-drafted flows the brain decides whether to send, drafts, and scores the draft for value.\n5. The message lands in the approval queue, or skips it if the flow's approval mode is `auto`.\n6. The drain worker picks up approved messages, checks compliance gates (postal address set, signing key present, production URL sane) and the throttle (suppression, frequency caps, send window), injects one-click unsubscribe headers and the postal footer, and hands the message to your transport.\n7. With Resend, opens, clicks, bounces, and complaints come back through the webhook and appear on the message and contact. Feedback only moves forward (`sent` → `opened` → `clicked`), never backward."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#4",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > How an event becomes an email",
    "text": "**Timing you should expect.** On the happy path this takes seconds: workers poll every 2 seconds and each stage hands off to the next. AI-drafted content adds LLM latency (three calls, typically seconds to a minute). Steps with delays wait for the delay, re-checked on the 15-minute scan. Lifecycle-transition and segment triggers are evaluated on the same 15-minute scan, so they can lag an event by up to 15 minutes. If a queued job is ever lost, 15-minute sweeps catch everything; the worst case from event to send is roughly 35 minutes."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#5",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Compilation: prompts in, deterministic plans out",
    "text": "A prompt-defined flow starts as plain language:\nWhen a user signs up, send a welcome email immediately. If they have not activated after 2 days, send a getting-started guide. After 5 days, send a case study. Exit the flow as soon as they activate.\n\nPressing **Compile** sends the prompt to your configured LLM exactly once. The result is a JSON execution plan: an ordered list of steps with delays, conditions, send-window policies, and exit conditions, validated against a strict schema before it is stored. If the model produces something invalid, compilation fails safely and nothing changes.\n\nFrom then on the plan, not the prompt, is what runs. The engine reads it with SQL. The same prompt compiled twice produces equivalent plans, and the plan is inspectable in the flow editor, so there are no surprises at send time.\n\nRecompiling happens only when you ask (prompt changed, or a membership froze on an unrecognized condition). Editing a flow's prompt does nothing until you compile it again.\n\nRules that follow from this design:\n\n- Compilation and AI drafting need an LLM provider configured. Everything else does not.\n- The LLM never decides timing at send time. Delays, windows, and throttles are computed by the engine.\n- Conditions are a closed vocabulary (below). Plans cannot contain arbitrary predicates."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#6",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Compilation: prompts in, deterministic plans out",
    "text": "**Conditions inside a flow.** A step can carry a condition (\"send this email only if...\": false skips the step and the flow moves on) and an exit condition (\"stop the whole flow instead if...\"), and a plan can carry exits that are checked before every step. A condition is one check, or an `all` / `any` group of checks (nesting up to 3 deep, at most 10 checks in total):\n\n| Check | Examples |\n|---|---|\n| Lifecycle stage | is / is not `at_risk` |\n| Payment status, plan, engagement, has an email | `paid`, plan is `Pro`, engagement is not `minimal` |\n| A custom trait (any key you send in `identify`) | `seats` greater than 10, `country` is `Ghana`, `beta` is set |\n| Something they did | did / did not `exported_report`, in the last N days, since reaching this step, or since entering the flow; \"did\" can ask for at least N times |\n\nThese are the same contact checks a trigger audience uses (see *Audience* above), plus lifecycle stage and events. In the flow editor, a hand-written step has a *Conditions (optional)* section and the flow has *Stop the flow early*. The same shapes through the API, on `POST /v1/flows/:id/plan`:"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#7",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Compilation: prompts in, deterministic plans out",
    "text": "```json\n{ \"steps\": [ { \"order\": 1, \"delay\": \"0m\", \"action_type\": \"nurture_reactivate\", \"window_policy\": \"respect_window\",\n    \"subject\": \"...\", \"body_html\": \"...\",\n    \"condition\": { \"all\": [\n      { \"field\": \"payment_status\", \"op\": \"is\", \"value\": \"paid\" },\n      { \"field\": \"event\", \"name\": \"exported_report\", \"op\": \"not_performed\", \"within_days\": 7 } ] } } ],\n  \"exit_conditions\": [ { \"condition\": { \"field\": \"event\", \"name\": \"upgraded\", \"op\": \"performed\", \"since\": \"flow\" } } ] }\n```\n\nThings to know:"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#8",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Compilation: prompts in, deterministic plans out",
    "text": "- **A plan-level exit on an event acts within seconds.** \"Exit when `upgraded` happens\" ends the contact's flow as soon as the event arrives. Step-level conditions and exits, and anything about a contact fact or lifecycle stage, are checked when the step comes due or on the 15-minute scan.\n- **An event check needs a window.** Say `within_days` (back from now), `since: \"step\"` or `since: \"flow\"`. `since: \"step\"` has no meaning in a plan-level exit, where it counts from entering the flow.\n- **Checked when the step comes due**, not when the contact entered. Contact fields are read at that moment, so a contact who upgrades during a wait is judged on their plan then.\n- **A condition the engine cannot read does not guess.** The contact stays where they are and the reason is recorded on the membership (`condition_error`); it clears when the flow is fixed or recompiled. The API refuses to save one, so this only happens to hand-edited data.\n- **The three original shapes** (`lifecycle_state`, `lifecycle_state_not`, `event_since_step`) are unchanged and still valid anywhere a condition goes.\n- **Plain-English flows** use the same vocabulary: the AI is told these shapes when it compiles. It is told to use a custom trait only when your description names one."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#9",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Flows that split into paths",
    "text": "A flow is a straight line by default: every contact gets the same emails in the same order. A **split** sends different contacts down different paths. After the emails above it, the flow waits (or not), checks a condition, and sends the contact down the **If it holds** path or the **Otherwise** path. Each path is its own list of emails, and each can end in a split of its own.\nWelcome email. Wait 3 days. **Did they create a project?**\nYes: send \"Nice work, here is what to do next\".\nNo: send \"Stuck? Here is a quick guide\".\n\nIn the flow editor (for flows you write yourself), press **Split into two paths** under the last email. The condition is the same kind used everywhere else (see *Conditions inside a flow*): contact facts, lifecycle stage, and what they did, combined with all-of or any-of. The plan preview draws the paths as an outline. For plain-English flows, describe the fork in your prompt (see FLOW-PROMPTS.md) and the AI writes it.\n\nRules, which the editor enforces and the API checks:"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#10",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Flows that split into paths",
    "text": "- **A split is the last thing in its path.** When a path runs out of emails, the flow ends for that contact. An empty path is fine: \"otherwise, do nothing\".\n- **At most 3 splits on any one path, and 30 emails and splits in total.**\n- **No loops.** A contact can never be sent back to an earlier step, so nobody can be mailed forever. A plan with a loop, a path to a step that does not exist, or a step nothing leads to is refused with a message saying which.\n- **The wait counts from arriving at the split**, not from the previous email. The check happens when that wait is over, and what the contact did since arriving can be asked with \"since this step\".\n- **The decision is made once.** Contacts are not moved between paths later if the answer changes.\n- **A decision never sends an email by itself.** The first email on the chosen path goes out straight away if it is due; later ones follow their own delays.\n- **A step's own condition is different from a split.** \"Send this email only if...\" skips just that email and moves on. A split chooses a whole path.\n- **Straight-line flows are unchanged.** A flow without a split is saved exactly as before."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#11",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Flows that split into paths",
    "text": "The same through the API, on `POST /v1/flows/:id/plan`. A plan with splits has `version: 2`. Emails and splits share one numbering (`order`), the flow starts at order 1, and each email says what comes `next` (another order, or `\"end\"`). A split has a `delay`, a `condition`, and `then` / `else` targets:\n\n```json\n{ \"version\": 2,\n  \"steps\": [\n    { \"order\": 1, \"delay\": \"0m\", \"action_type\": \"onboard_welcome\", \"window_policy\": \"immediate\",\n      \"subject\": \"Welcome\", \"body_html\": \"<p>...</p>\", \"next\": 2 },\n    { \"order\": 3, \"delay\": \"0m\", \"action_type\": \"nurture_value\", \"window_policy\": \"respect_window\",\n      \"subject\": \"Nice work\", \"body_html\": \"<p>...</p>\", \"next\": \"end\" },\n    { \"order\": 4, \"delay\": \"0m\", \"action_type\": \"onboard_guide\", \"window_policy\": \"respect_window\",\n      \"subject\": \"Stuck?\", \"body_html\": \"<p>...</p>\", \"next\": \"end\" } ],\n  \"branches\": [\n    { \"order\": 2, \"delay\": \"3d\",\n      \"condition\": { \"field\": \"event\", \"name\": \"project_created\", \"op\": \"performed\", \"since\": \"flow\" },\n      \"then\": 3, \"else\": 4 } ] }\n```"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#12",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Flows that split into paths > Waiting for something to happen",
    "text": "A split can also **wait for an event instead of waiting a fixed time.** In the editor, choose *Wait for it to happen, up to a limit* on a split. The time you pick becomes the longest the contact will wait.\nWelcome email. **Wait up to 3 days for \"created a project\".**\nThey did, at any point in those 3 days: send \"Nice work\", straight away.\n3 days passed and they did not: send \"Stuck? Here is a quick guide\"."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#13",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Flows that split into paths > Waiting for something to happen",
    "text": "- **An event ends the wait within seconds.** When the event arrives, Loomail looks at the contacts waiting on exactly that event and moves them on straight away. Conditions about a contact fact (payment status, plan, a trait) or a lifecycle stage are not events, so those are checked on the 15-minute scan instead. If a background job is ever lost, the scan catches up. When the time runs out the normal check decides, so something that happens on the last day still counts.\n- **Use \"since this step\" for events** so only what happens after the contact reached the wait counts. With \"within the last N days\" an event from before they arrived can end the wait at once.\n- **It works for any condition,** not just events: \"wait up to 7 days for payment status to become paid\" is the same thing.\n- **The limit is required, from 1 minute up to 90 days.** Nobody waits forever.\n- **Everything else about splits applies:** the decision is made once, a split is the last thing in its path, no loops, and the same node limits. A condition the engine cannot read is reported while waiting, not guessed.\n- **Through the API:** add `\"early\": true` to the branch. Its `delay` is the longest to wait. A branch without `early` is unchanged."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#14",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Flows that split into paths > Waiting for something to happen",
    "text": "```json\n{ \"order\": 2, \"delay\": \"3d\", \"early\": true,\n  \"condition\": { \"field\": \"event\", \"name\": \"project_created\", \"op\": \"performed\", \"since\": \"step\" },\n  \"then\": 3, \"else\": 4 }\n```"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#15",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Flows that split into paths > A/B tests and holdouts",
    "text": "A split can also send people down a path **at random** instead of by a condition. In the editor, on a split, choose *A random split (A/B test or holdout)* and pick the share. Each person is assigned once, by a fixed hash of their place in the flow, and stays in that version.\nWelcome email. **Split 50/50:**\nVersion A: send \"Welcome to Acme\".\nVersion B: send \"Here's your first win\".\nGoal: created a project. Result: A 22%, B 31%."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#16",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Flows that split into paths > A/B tests and holdouts",
    "text": "- **A/B test:** put different emails on the two paths and compare. Set a **goal** on the flow to compare how many people from each version get there. Opens and clicks are shown too, for each version's own emails only (emails before the split went to everyone and are left out). Opens are approximate, because some mail apps load images automatically.\n- **Holdout:** leave one path empty and give the other most people, for example 90/10. The 10% are held back and sent nothing, so comparing them with everyone else is the one honest way to see whether the emails made a difference. The goal keeps checking held-back people until their window ends, so the comparison is fair.\n- **The verdict is careful.** The flow page says \"too early to tell\" until each version has at least 30 people and at least 10 people in total have reached the goal. After that it uses a standard two-sample test: \"Version B is probably better\" appears only when chance is unlikely to explain the gap (p under 0.05), otherwise \"no clear difference yet\". Small tests mislead, so check back as numbers grow.\n- **Rules:** one split is two versions, with the share for version A from 1 to 99 percent. A random split cannot also be a wait-until, and it follows the same limits as any split: no loops, at most 3 splits on a path, 30 emails and splits in total."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#17",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Flows that split into paths > A/B tests and holdouts",
    "text": "- **A person never changes version.** Editing the percentage later affects only people who reach the split afterwards. Someone who enters the flow again is a new entry and may be assigned again.\n- **There is no one-click \"send everyone the winner\" yet.** Edit the flow yourself: remove the split and keep the winning path's emails.\n- **Plain-English flows:** say \"send half of them X and half Y\", \"test two subject lines\" or \"hold back 10% as a control\", and the AI writes a random split.\n- **Through the API:** a branch with `\"split\": { \"percent_a\": 50 }` instead of a `condition`. A holdout has `\"else\": \"end\"`. `GET /v1/flows/:id/results` returns a `split_tests` list with the numbers and verdict for each split."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#18",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Flows that split into paths > A/B tests and holdouts",
    "text": "```json\n{ \"order\": 2, \"delay\": \"0m\", \"split\": { \"percent_a\": 90 }, \"then\": 3, \"else\": \"end\" }\n```\n\nThe API accepts more shapes than the editor builds: paths may rejoin (two emails can both lead to a shared one), and a step can jump over others with `next`. The plan preview shows these as \"Continues at step N\". The editor itself only builds the tree shape above."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#19",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Calling your own system: webhook steps",
    "text": "A flow does not have to send an email at every step. A **webhook step** calls a web address of yours instead: tell your\nCRM, post to Slack, open a ticket, start something in your product. Add one in a person-written flow with **Add\nwebhook** next to **Add step**. It has a delay, a web address, and the same optional conditions as an email step\n(\"call only if\", \"stop the flow instead if\"), and it can sit anywhere in a sequence or on either path of a split.\nIt sends no email.\n\n**What your server receives.** A `POST` with a JSON body:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"f0df51c9-2a63-44c8-ac62-7cc3bf9b8b37\",\n  \"type\": \"flow.webhook\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-17T20:34:22.550Z\",\n  \"attempt\": 1,\n  \"flow\": { \"id\": \"...\", \"name\": \"Welcome and notify\" },\n  \"step\": 2,\n  \"contact\": {\n    \"id\": \"...\", \"user_id\": \"user_42\", \"email\": \"ada@example.com\", \"name\": \"Ada Lovelace\",\n    \"company\": \"Analytical Engines\", \"lifecycle_state\": \"engaged\", \"payment_status\": \"free\",\n    \"properties\": { \"seats\": 4, \"city\": \"London\" }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHeaders: `X-Loomail-Delivery` (the same id as `id`, identical on every retry: use it to ignore repeats),\n`X-Loomail-Event: flow.webhook`, and `X-Loomail-Signature: t=<unix seconds>,v1=<hex>`."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#20",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Calling your own system: webhook steps",
    "text": "**Check the signature.** `v1` is `HMAC-SHA256(secret, t + \".\" + raw body)`, hex. Recompute it over the body exactly as\nreceived, compare in constant time, and reject a `t` more than a few minutes old. The secret is one per workspace;\na workspace owner sees it (and can rotate it) under **Check that calls come from Loomail** on any flow with a webhook\nstep. Rotating replaces it at once, so update your server at the same time. The editor shows a Node.js example.\n\n**Answering.** Reply `2xx` within 10 seconds. A timeout, a network error, `408`, `429` or any `5xx` is tried again\nafter 1 minute, 5 minutes, 30 minutes, 2 hours and 12 hours (six attempts in all, about 15 hours). Any other answer\n(a `4xx`, a redirect) is final: Loomail records it and does not ask again. Redirects are never followed. Calls are made\nat least once, so build the receiver to cope with a repeat.\n\n**See what happened.** Below the editor, **Webhook calls** shows how many were delivered, are waiting or failed over the\nlast 30 days, and the latest 50 with the answer or the reason. A paused flow holds its calls (they go out when you resume);\narchiving the flow fails them."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#21",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Goals: did the flow work?",
    "text": "A **goal** says what success looks like for a flow, so Loomail can tell you how many people who entered it got there. Set it on any flow, from the *Goal* card on the flow page. It can be changed at any time, even on an active flow, with no recompile.\nFlow: \"Welcome\". **Goal: Created a project**, counted if it happens within 30 days of entering.\n\nA goal has a name, a condition (the same vocabulary as everywhere else: an event, a contact fact, a lifecycle stage, or all-of / any-of), a window of 1 to 90 days (default 30), and a choice of whether to **leave the flow** when it is reached (default yes, so someone who already succeeded is not sent \"Stuck? Here is a quick guide\").\n\nWhat you see on the flow page and in the Flows list:\n\n- **Entered**, **reached the goal**, the **conversion rate**, and the **typical time** to get there (the median), for people who entered in the last 30 days.\n- **Per email:** how many people were sent each email, and how many of those reached the goal.\n\nHow it works, and what it does not tell you:"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#22",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Goals: did the flow work?",
    "text": "- **Event goals are recorded within seconds; other goals on the 15-minute scan.** When the goal's event arrives, the contact is checked at once, whether they are still in the flow or already finished it (until their window ends, so \"converted a week after the last email\" counts). A goal on a contact fact such as payment status is checked on the scan. A conversion is recorded once per person per entry.\n- **Use \"since entering the flow\" for events.** Otherwise something that happened before they joined can count. A fact like \"payment status is paid\" also counts people who were already paid when they joined.\n- **It shows who got there after joining, not that the emails caused it.** Proving cause needs a holdout: a random split where a share of people are deliberately sent nothing (see *A/B tests and holdouts*). Compare them with everyone else.\n- **Changing a goal changes the future, not the past.** Conversions already recorded keep the goal name they had. Removing the goal stops new conversions but keeps the history.\n- **The older plan-level exit condition** still works unchanged. A goal with \"leave the flow\" does the same job and also counts the result, so for new flows prefer a goal.\n- **Plain-English flows:** the AI does not write goals. Set the goal on the flow page after it is created."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#23",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Goals: did the flow work?",
    "text": "- **Through the API:** `goal` on `POST` and `PATCH /v1/flows` (`null` removes it), `GET /v1/flows/:id/results?days=30` for the numbers, and `entered_30d`, `converted_30d` and `has_goal` in `GET /v1/flows/stats`."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#24",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Goals: did the flow work?",
    "text": "```json\n{ \"goal\": { \"name\": \"Created a project\",\n            \"condition\": { \"field\": \"event\", \"name\": \"project_created\", \"op\": \"performed\", \"since\": \"flow\" },\n            \"window_days\": 30, \"exit_on_conversion\": true } }\n```"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#25",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > The approval queue",
    "text": "AI-drafted flows default to `approval_mode: require`: every generated message waits in **Approvals** until a human approves or rejects it. This is the trust mechanism. The AI writes, you publish.\n\n- Approve sends within seconds; reject discards permanently. Bulk approve/reject handles up to 100 at a time.\n- Unapproved messages wait forever. Nothing auto-sends, nothing expires.\n- Fixed-content flows default to `auto` because a human already wrote the copy.\n- Approval mode is per flow. A common pattern: `require` while a flow is new, switch to `auto` once you trust its output.\n- Messages that fail generation land in `failed` with the reason attached, and can be retried from the queue."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#26",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Three ways to produce email content",
    "text": "1. **AI-drafted** (`ai_drafted`). Each step carries an instruction for the drafter. Per contact per step, the brain runs decide (send, wait, or skip), draft (write the email), and assess (is this worth sending; if not, the message is value-gated and dies quietly). Personalization comes from the contact's events, traits, lifecycle state, and your knowledge base entries. Requires an LLM provider.\n2. **Human-written** (`fixed_content`). You write the subject and body for each step in the flow editor; they are stored as templates with variable interpolation. No LLM involved at any point. This is how the library flow works, and it is the recommended way to get comfortable before turning on AI drafting.\n3. **AI-assisted human copy**. While editing a fixed-content step, you can ask the AI for a draft of that step, edit it, and save it as your own. The AI contributes once, at writing time; the result is ordinary fixed content. Requires an LLM provider.\n\nYou can mix approaches across flows, and reference your own templates (`@template:name`) and knowledge base entries (`@kb:name`) inside prompts."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#27",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Flow mechanics",
    "text": "**Triggers.** Four types, three of which enroll today:\n\n| Trigger | Config | Enrolls |\n|---|---|---|\n| `event` | an event name, e.g. `signed_up` | Yes, near-instant. Only `track` calls trigger flows; `identify` does not |\n| `lifecycle_transition` | from-state, to-state | Yes, on the 15-minute scan |\n| `segment` | a saved segment (`{segment_id}`), or one cell of the retention grid (`{tenure_bucket, recency_bucket}`) | Yes, on the 15-minute scan, capped at 200 enrollments per flow per scan |\n| `manual` | - | Accepted by the API but not wired to enrollment yet |\n\nA segment trigger starts the flow for everyone who is in the segment now and anyone who joins later. The rules are read at each scan, so editing a segment changes who enters from then on; people already in the flow stay. The older form, one **retention-grid cell** (tenure `new`, `growing`, `established`, `loyal` crossed with recency `active`, `cooling`, `idle`, `dormant`), still works unchanged and is what the Analytics grid links to."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#28",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Segments",
    "text": "A **segment** is a saved description of a group of people (\"paid customers not seen in 14 days\"), not a fixed list. Loomail works out who matches whenever it is asked. Build them under **Segments**, or press *Save as segment* on the People page.\n\nA segment has a match mode (**all** or **any**) and up to 20 rules in total. One level of nested groups is allowed, with up to 10 rules per group, so \"paid AND (power OR regular)\" is expressible. Rules can be about:\n\n- **Contact facts**: payment status, engagement depth, plan, has an email, or a custom trait.\n- **Lifecycle stage**: is, is not, or is one of.\n- **First or last seen**: within, or more than, N days ago.\n- **What they did**: performed or did not perform an event in the last N days (up to 365), optionally at least N times.\n\nThings to know:"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#29",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Segments",
    "text": "- **Counts are cached.** The list shows the count from the last time it was worked out, with the time; press recount to refresh. Counting stops at 100,000 (shown as \"100,000+\") and a count that takes more than a few seconds is refused rather than left running.\n- **A flow that uses a segment pins it.** A segment cannot be archived while a flow starts from it; the API (422) and the page name the flows.\n- **Suppressed people never enroll**, and the usual re-entry and concurrency settings of the flow still apply. Candidates are filtered before the 200-per-scan cap so blocked people cannot starve the rest.\n- **Event rules on a name never sent** match nobody (or everybody, for \"did not\"); the editor warns when the name has never been seen.\n- **Through the API**: `GET/POST /v1/segments`, `GET/PATCH/DELETE /v1/segments/:id`, `POST /v1/segments/:id/refresh`, `POST /v1/segments/preview`, and `GET /v1/contacts?segment_id=`.\n\n**Audience (optional).** An `event` or `lifecycle_transition` trigger can carry an audience: \"only enroll contacts where...\". In the flow editor it is the *Only for certain contacts* section, and unlike the trigger it can be changed at any time, even on an active flow, without recompiling. Up to 5 conditions, all of which must hold:"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#30",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Segments",
    "text": "| Condition | Operators |\n|---|---|\n| Payment status (`free`, `trial`, `paid`, `past_due`, `cancelled`) | is, is not, is one of |\n| Engagement (`power`, `regular`, `casual`, `minimal`) | is, is not, is one of |\n| Plan (the `plan` trait, when it is a plan name) | is, is not |\n| Email address | has one / has none |\n| A custom trait (any key you send in `identify`) | is, is not, contains, greater than, less than, is set, is not set |\n\nThe same shape through the API, in `trigger_config`:\n\n```json\n{ \"from\": \"engaged\", \"to\": \"at_risk\",\n  \"audience\": { \"conditions\": [\n    { \"field\": \"payment_status\", \"op\": \"is\", \"value\": \"paid\" },\n    { \"field\": \"plan\", \"op\": \"is\", \"value\": \"Pro\" } ] } }\n```\n\nThings to know:"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#31",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Segments",
    "text": "- **It is checked at the moment the trigger fires.** A contact who does not match then is not enrolled for that occurrence, and is not enrolled later if they start matching. Contacts already in the flow are not affected when you change the audience.\n- **Traits must be there when the event arrives.** For event triggers, send `identify` before `track` (the server SDKs do). A `track` that beats its `identify` is judged on the traits known so far.\n- **Text comparisons ignore case.** A missing trait never matches \"is\", \"contains\", \"greater than\" or \"less than\", and always matches \"is not\". Numbers stored as text (`\"12\"`) compare as numbers.\n- **A filter the engine cannot read enrolls nobody** (it fails closed) and logs a warning. The API refuses to save one, so this only happens to hand-edited data.\n- **Not for segment (retention grid) triggers.** The API rejects an audience there.\n- The audience is not shown to the AI that compiles or drafts the flow; the engine enforces it.\n\n**Classes.** Every flow is `nurture` or `critical`. A contact can be in at most one active nurture flow at a time; a higher-priority flow evicts the current one. Critical flows (dunning, limits) have no concurrency limit and bypass the frequency caps and send window. Nothing bypasses the suppression list."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#32",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Segments",
    "text": "**Re-entry.** Per flow: `once`, `cooldown` (default 30 days), or `every_time`. Enforced by a uniqueness constraint on active memberships, so races cannot double-enroll.\n\n**Send windows.** Each step is `immediate` (welcome, dunning: ignores the send window) or `respect_window` (waits for 09:00-17:00 weekdays, or whatever you configure). Frequency caps (1/day, 2/week, 48h gap by default) apply to everything except critical flows.\n\n---"
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#33",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Guides > Write your first prompt-defined flow",
    "text": "1. Configure an LLM provider (Settings → LLM, or `loomail llm set`). Without one, use fixed content instead: steps 2-4 still work, you just skip compilation and write each step's copy yourself.\n2. Flows → New flow. Choose the trigger (an event name your app sends, a lifecycle transition, or a retention-grid cell) and write the prompt. FLOW-PROMPTS.md has working starting points.\n3. Compile. Read the generated plan in the editor; check the delays, conditions, and exit conditions match your intent. Fix the prompt, recompile.\n4. Activate the flow. New matching contacts enroll from that moment.\n5. Watch **Approvals** for the first messages. Approve a few, and switch the flow to auto-approve when you trust it.\n\nVerify it works by firing the trigger at yourself, exactly like steps 9-11 of QUICKSTART.md."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#34",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Guides > Set up open and click tracking (Resend only)",
    "text": "1. Save your Resend transport first; note your tenant ID (see INSTALLATION.md).\n2. In the Resend dashboard, create a webhook pointing at `https://<your-host>/webhooks/resend/<tenantId>` and subscribe to the `email.*` events.\n3. Copy the webhook's signing secret into your transport config (`webhook_secret` field, Settings → Transport or `loomail transport set`).\n4. Opens, clicks, bounces, and complaints now appear on messages and contacts. Permanent bounces and complaints also add the address to your suppression list automatically.\n\nSMTP transports have no feedback channel; there is nothing to configure."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#35",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Guides > Import a suppression list from your old provider",
    "text": "Settings → Sent → Suppressions → Import, paste one address per line (up to 10,000). Or:\n\n```bash\ncurl -X POST http://localhost:3000/v1/suppressions/import \\\n  -H \"Cookie: loomail_session=<your-session>\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"addresses\":[\"old-unsub-1@example.com\",\"old-unsub-2@example.com\"]}'\n```\n\nSuppressed addresses are blocked at enrollment and again at send, for every flow including critical ones. Transactional auth email (login links, invites) is the only exception."
  },
  {
    "id": "CONCEPTS#36",
    "source": "Concepts guide",
    "heading": "Concepts and Guides > Guides > Apply a business model template",
    "text": "If your product is a freemium, time-limited trial, or preview-free SaaS, applying the matching template (offered during first-run setup, or via the API) seeds sensible lifecycle tuning, throttle settings, and a set of draft flows with pre-written prompts for your model. The flows arrive as drafts: review their prompts, compile, and activate the ones you want. Templates can be applied once per workspace.\n\n---\n\nNext: FLOW-PROMPTS.md for a library of working flow prompts, FAQ.md for troubleshooting."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#0",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library",
    "text": "Working prompts for the flow editor. Each one names its trigger, its prompt, what the compiler produces from it, and when to use it. Paste the prompt into a new flow, set the trigger as shown, compile, review the plan, activate. Every prompt on this page has been compiled successfully against the current compiler.\n\nBefore pasting, read CONCEPTS.md so the compiled plan does not surprise you."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#1",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > How to write a prompt that compiles well",
    "text": "The compiler is an LLM with a strict output contract. It handles natural language, but these habits produce the best plans:"
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#2",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > How to write a prompt that compiles well",
    "text": "- **Name the trigger event exactly** as your app sends it (`signed_up`, not `sign-up`). The trigger itself is set in the editor; the prompt should agree with it.\n- **Number the steps with their delays**: \"Step 1 (day 0, immediate) ... Step 2 (2 days later, respect_window) ...\". Delays are minutes, hours, or days only.\n- **Say `immediate` or `respect_window` per step.** Immediate ignores the send window (use for welcomes, payment failures); respect_window holds the email for the next 09:00-17:00 weekday slot.\n- **Use the closed condition vocabulary.** Steps can proceed-if or exit-if on: the lifecycle stage; payment status, plan, engagement, whether they have an email; a custom trait you name (like `seats`); and whether they did or did not do an event, in the last N days, since the step, or since entering the flow. Checks can be combined with all-of / any-of. Say which ones you mean (\"only if they are on a paid plan and have not exported a report in 7 days\"). Nothing outside this list compiles. See *Conditions inside a flow* in CONCEPTS.md.\n- **Describe different paths with \"if ... otherwise ...\"** and a wait. See Different paths for different contacts.\n- **State the exit conditions**: \"Exit the flow the moment the user activates\" compiles to a plan-level exit on the lifecycle change.\n- **End with class and re-entry**: \"Flow class: nurture. Reentry: once.\""
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#3",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > How to write a prompt that compiles well",
    "text": "Limits to know before writing:\n\n- No schedule or cron triggers. A prompt cannot say \"every Monday\"; enrollment needs an event, a lifecycle transition, or a retention-grid segment.\n- `manual` triggers are accepted but do not enroll anyone yet.\n- Conditions cannot reference plan names, prices, or arbitrary traits. Move product facts into Settings → Brain context (`brain_context`) or a knowledge base entry and reference it with `@kb:name`.\n- Your own fixed templates can be referenced with `@template:name`."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#4",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > What the AI can see when it writes each email",
    "text": "Compiling turns your prompt into steps. Later, for each contact and each step, the AI writes the email, and then a quality check reads the draft against the step's instruction. If the draft misses something the instruction demanded, the email is **held back** (status `value_gated`) rather than sent generic. So a prompt must only ask for things the AI is actually given.\n\n**The AI is given, for every email:**\n\n- the contact's `name`, `email` and `company`\n- the **name of their plan** (for example `Growth`), if you send it as the `plan` trait when you identify the user\n- their lifecycle state, how long they have been a contact, and their engagement depth\n- their payment status: `free`, `trial`, `paid`, `past_due` or `cancelled`\n- how active they were this week compared with last week\n- when they were last emailed, and what kind of email it was\n- the **names** of their recent events and the features they use most (names only)\n- your Brain context (Settings) and any knowledge-base entries that match the step\n\n**The AI is not given:**"
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#5",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > What the AI can see when it writes each email",
    "text": "- the **details inside an event**: an amount, an order, a product or page name. Event properties are deliberately kept out of the prompt (they are free text from your users' side and can be large).\n- custom fields you send with `identify`, such as `first_name` or `job_title`. Only `name`, `email`, `company`, `plan` and the payment status are used. Send `name` (for example \"Ama Boateng\"), not `first_name`.\n\n| Works | Does not |\n|---|---|\n| \"Congratulate them by name, mention their company, and suggest one next step.\" | \"Mention the amount they paid.\" (the amount is inside the event) |\n| \"Thank them for upgrading and mention their plan.\" (after you identify them with `plan: \"Growth\"`) | \"Confirm which plan they chose\" when the plan is only inside the upgrade event |\n| \"Thank them for upgrading.\" | \"Reference the last page they visited.\" |\n| \"Remind them they have been with us since signing up.\" | \"Mention their order total.\" |\n| \"If they are on a trial, mention it ends soon.\" (payment status) | \"Say their trial ends on 14 October.\" (a date from your app) |\n\nTo name the plan in an upgrade email, send the new plan when you identify the user, **before or together with** the upgrade event (`identify` with `traits: { plan: \"Growth\" }`), and write \"mention their plan\". Sending it only inside the event does not work."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#6",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > What the AI can see when it writes each email",
    "text": "If you need a fact the AI is not given, put it where it is: a product fact that is the same for everyone (what each plan includes) goes in **Brain context** or a **knowledge-base entry**; a fact that differs per contact and is not in the list above (an order total, a renewal date) cannot be used in an AI-drafted email today. For that, write the email yourself with a fixed template (`@template:name`), which can use the contact's fields directly.\n\nOne more rule that surprises people: a contact can be in only **one nurture flow at a time** (a higher-priority flow can take over). A contact still waiting on the day-3 step of one nurture flow is not enrolled in another.\n\n---"
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#7",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Onboarding and activation > Welcome sequence",
    "text": "**Trigger:** event `signed_up`\n\n```\nSend a 3-step welcome sequence to new signups.\nStep 1 (0 minutes, immediate): Welcome them, thank them for signing up, and point\nthem at the single most valuable first action in the product.\nStep 2 (1 day later, respect_window): A short getting-started guide: the two or\nthree things successful users do in their first week, with links.\nStep 3 (3 days later, respect_window): Share a concrete example or mini case study\nof what a user achieved, and invite them to reply with questions.\nExit the flow if the user activates (lifecycle_state changes to activated).\nFlow class: nurture. Reentry: once.\n```\n\n**Produces:** three steps at 0m / 1d / 3d with the welcome step immediate and the rest windowed; exit on activation. This mirrors the prebuilt library flow, which ships with hand-written copy and needs no LLM; write your own prompt when you want AI-drafted copy or different timing.\n\n**Use when:** you have exactly one obvious first-run journey. If you are unsure, install the library flow from the Home checklist instead."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#8",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Onboarding and activation > Activation rescue",
    "text": "**Trigger:** event `signed_up`\n\n```\nSend an activation rescue sequence to users who signed up but have not activated.\nStep 1 (2 days, respect_window): Only send if the user is still in lifecycle state\nsigned_up. Remind them of the one action that unlocks value and make it one click away.\nStep 2 (3 days later, respect_window): Only send if the user has still not activated.\nOffer help directly: ask what is blocking them and invite a reply.\nExit the flow if the user activates.\nFlow class: nurture. Reentry: once.\n```\n\n**Produces:** two proceed-if-gated steps using `lifecycle_state` conditions, exit on `activated`. Contacts who activate on their own silently skip the whole flow.\n\n**Use when:** your activation rate is the metric that matters; this recovers the users the welcome sequence did not reach."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#9",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Revenue > Trial ending",
    "text": "**Trigger:** event `trial_ending` (sent by your billing system a few days before expiry)\n\n```\nSend a trial expiry sequence.\nStep 1 (0 minutes, immediate): Warn that the trial ends soon, summarize what they\nhave accomplished during the trial using their usage data, and show the upgrade path.\nStep 2 (2 days later, immediate): Final warning before expiry. Be direct about what\nstops working, the price to keep it, and make upgrading one click.\nExit the flow if the user upgrades (event subscription_started occurs).\nFlow class: critical. Reentry: once.\n```\n\n**Produces:** two immediate steps; exit when your `subscription_started` event fires. Critical class means these bypass the send window and frequency caps, which is what you want for money-on-the-line email.\n\n**Use when:** trials have a hard expiry. Your billing system must fire `trial_ending` (and `subscription_started` on conversion); see INGESTION.md."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#10",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Revenue > Failed payment recovery (dunning)",
    "text": "**Trigger:** event `payment_failed`\n\n```\nSend a payment recovery sequence.\nStep 1 (0 minutes, immediate): Notify that the payment failed, say plainly that no\naction is needed yet if they are already updating their card, and link to the\nbilling page. Tone: helpful, not alarming.\nStep 2 (2 days later, immediate): Only send if the event payment_recovered has not\noccurred since step 1. Second notice: state that the account will lose access soon\nand link to update payment details.\nStep 3 (3 days later, immediate): Only send if payment_recovered has still not\noccurred. Final notice with a specific date after which the subscription cancels.\nExit the flow if the event payment_recovered occurs.\nFlow class: critical. Reentry: every_time.\n```\n\n**Produces:** three immediate critical steps gated by `event_since_step: payment_recovered`, plus a plan-level exit on the same event. Re-entry `every_time` because cards fail more than once.\n\n**Use when:** you have recurring billing. Fire `payment_failed` and `payment_recovered` from your billing webhook handler."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#11",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Revenue > Usage limit approaching",
    "text": "**Trigger:** event `usage_limit_approaching`\n\n```\nSend a usage limit notification.\nStep 1 (0 minutes, immediate): Tell the user they are close to their plan limit,\nshow how much they have used, explain what happens at the limit, and present the\nupgrade as the way to keep going. One email, no sequence.\nExit the flow if the user upgrades (event subscription_started occurs).\nFlow class: critical. Reentry: cooldown 30 days.\n```\n\n**Produces:** a single immediate critical step.\n\n**Use when:** your pricing has metered limits. Fire the event from your metering code when a customer crosses roughly 80% of their allowance."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#12",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Retention > Win-back",
    "text": "**Trigger:** lifecycle transition `engaged` → `dormant`\n\n```\nSend a win-back email to users who have gone dormant.\nStep 1 (0 minutes, immediate): Acknowledge the gap without guilt-tripping,\nreference something specific they did before going quiet, and give one concrete\nreason to come back today. Do not pitch an upgrade.\nStep 2 (5 days later, respect_window): Only send if the user has not returned\n(lifecycle_state is still dormant). Share what is new since they left.\nExit the flow if the user returns (lifecycle_state changes to engaged).\nFlow class: nurture. Reentry: cooldown 30 days.\n```\n\n**Produces:** two steps, the first immediate, the second gated on still-dormant, exit on return to engaged.\n\n**Use when:** you want a safety net under your most engaged cohort. Lifecycle transitions are evaluated on the 15-minute scan, so dormancy triggers can lag by up to 15 minutes; irrelevant at this timescale."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#13",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Retention > Churn-risk check-in",
    "text": "**Trigger:** lifecycle transition `engaged` → `at_risk`\n\n```\nSend a gentle check-in when a previously engaged user becomes at risk.\nStep 1 (0 minutes, respect_window): A short personal note: ask how the product is\nworking for them, mention one feature they use most based on their usage data, and\ninvite a direct reply. No links to upgrade, no pressure.\nExit the flow if the user becomes engaged again.\nFlow class: nurture. Reentry: cooldown 30 days.\n```\n\n**Produces:** one windowed step, exit on recovery.\n\n**Use when:** you would rather catch churn at the `at_risk` stage than win back the `dormant`."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#14",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Expansion > Upgrade nudge after activation",
    "text": "**Trigger:** lifecycle transition `signed_up` → `activated`\n\n```\nSend an upgrade suggestion to freshly activated free users.\nStep 1 (7 days, respect_window): Congratulate them on getting value, show what the\npaid plan adds on top of what they already use, and make the upgrade one click.\nKeep it helpful, not pushy.\nExit the flow if the user upgrades (event subscription_started occurs).\nFlow class: nurture. Reentry: once.\n```\n\n**Produces:** one windowed step delayed 7 days after activation.\n\n**Use when:** free-to-paid conversion rides on demonstrated value rather than trial pressure."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#15",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Expansion > Re-engage idle established accounts",
    "text": "**Trigger:** segment `established` x `idle` (retention grid cell)\n\n```\nSend a re-engagement email to long-tenured accounts that have gone idle.\nStep 1 (0 minutes, respect_window): Remind them what they have built in the product,\nsummarize recent improvements they have not seen, and offer one low-effort action\nthat delivers value in minutes.\nFlow class: nurture. Reentry: cooldown 30 days.\n```\n\n**Produces:** one step aimed at a grid cell. Segment enrollment runs on the 15-minute scan and is capped at 200 contacts per flow per scan, so large cells drain over several scans.\n\n**Use when:** you want to target an audience by tenure and recency rather than by a single event. The retention grid in Analytics previews exactly who the cell contains.\n\n---"
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#16",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Different paths for different contacts",
    "text": "When the flow should do something different depending on the contact, say so in plain words and the AI writes a split (see *Flows that split into paths* in CONCEPTS.md). Use the words \"if\", \"otherwise\" and a wait: the AI only branches when the description clearly needs it. A plain sequence of emails, even with \"only send if...\" on some of them, stays a straight line."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#17",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Different paths for different contacts",
    "text": "- **Say what decides the path**, using something Loomail knows: what they did (\"created a project\"), their plan or payment status, their lifecycle stage, or a trait you send (name it, such as `seats`).\n- **Say how long to wait** before deciding, so the check has something to look at.\n- **Say what each path sends**, and what happens on a path that sends nothing (\"otherwise, stop\").\n- Keep it small: a path can pass through at most 3 splits.\n- **To test two versions, say so:** \"send half of them X and half Y\" or \"test two subject lines\" writes a random split (A/B test). \"Hold back 10% as a control\" writes a holdout. Set a goal on the flow afterwards to compare the results. See *A/B tests and holdouts* in CONCEPTS.md.\n- **To wait for something, say \"wait up to ... for ...\"** (for example \"wait up to 3 days for them to create a project; if they do, send X, otherwise send Y\"). The AI writes a wait-until split: the contact moves on within seconds of the event, or when the time is up. A plain \"wait 3 days, then check\" is an ordinary split."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#18",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Different paths for different contacts > Onboarding that adapts to what they did",
    "text": "When a user signs up, send a welcome email straight away. Three days later, if they have created a project, send a tips email about getting more from projects. Otherwise, send a short email offering help getting started, and then stop.\n\n**Produces:** a welcome email, a split after 3 days on \"did `project_created` since entering the flow\", a tips email on the yes path, a help email on the other path, and the flow ends on both paths. Your app must send the `project_created` event for the check to mean anything."
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#19",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > Different paths for different contacts > Treat paying and free contacts differently",
    "text": "When a contact goes from engaged to at risk, wait 2 days. If they are on a paid plan, send a personal check-in from the founder. If not, send a win-back email with a reminder of what the free plan includes.\n\n**Produces:** a split on payment status, with one email on each path. (If you only ever want one of these two groups in the flow at all, an *audience* on the trigger is simpler: see CONCEPTS.md.)"
  },
  {
    "id": "FLOW-PROMPTS#20",
    "source": "Flow prompt library",
    "heading": "Flow Prompt Library > After you paste one in",
    "text": "1. Compile and read the plan. Check delays, which steps are immediate, and the exit conditions.\n2. Send the trigger event to a test contact with your own email address (the QUICKSTART.md steps 9-11 pattern) and watch the message arrive in Approvals.\n3. Approve a few sends. When the copy is consistently good, switch the flow to auto-approve.\n\nIf compilation fails, the error is shown on the flow. The common causes are a condition outside the closed vocabulary, a delay that is not in minutes/hours/days, or a trigger mismatch between the editor and the prompt."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#0",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail",
    "text": "Audience: anyone integrating a product with a Loomail install. Everything on\nthis page is copy-paste runnable. Replace `https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST` with\nyour install's base URL (`http://localhost:3000` in the default compose\nsetup) and the example keys with your own."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#1",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > What \"Segment-compatible\" means here",
    "text": "Loomail accepts the Segment wire format for `track` and `identify`, single\ncalls and `/v1/batch`, with `messageId` (server-side dedup), `timestamp`\n(clamped to +/- 72 hours of server time), `traits` (shallow merge on\nidentify; explicit `null` clears a trait), and `properties`.\n\nNot supported: `page`, `screen`, `group` and `alias`. A `track` call may have\nan `anonymousId` instead of a `userId` (see \"Anonymous visitors\" below);\n`identify` always needs a `userId`. Loomail acts on product lifecycle events,\nnot web analytics."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#2",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > Keys",
    "text": "Two kinds, created in the dashboard under **Integrate** (or via the API,\nbelow):\n\n- **Publishable** (`mf_pub_...`): safe to paste into a web page. It can write\n  events and nothing else - there is no key-authenticated read endpoint at\n  all. Optional origin allowlist; rate limited to 300 requests/minute.\n- **Secret** (`mf_live_...`): your backend. Never put it in browser code,\n  a mobile app, or a public repo. Rate limited to 3000 requests/minute.\n\nRaw keys are shown once at creation. Only a SHA-256 hash is stored. Revoking\na key takes effect immediately."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#3",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 1. curl",
    "text": "```bash\ncurl -X POST https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST/v1/identify \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer mf_live_YOUR_SECRET_KEY\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"userId\": \"user_123\", \"traits\": {\"email\": \"user@example.com\", \"plan\": \"trial\"}}'\n\ncurl -X POST https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST/v1/track \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer mf_live_YOUR_SECRET_KEY\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n  -d '{\"userId\": \"user_123\", \"event\": \"signed_up\", \"messageId\": \"optional-dedup-key\"}'\n```"
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#4",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 2. The browser snippet",
    "text": "Served by your own install at `/loomail.js`. Paste before `</body>`:\n\n```html\n<script>\n  window.loomail = window.loomail || function () {\n    (window.loomail.q = window.loomail.q || []).push(arguments);\n  };\n</script>\n<script async src=\"https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST/loomail.js\"></script>\n<script>\n  loomail(\"init\", \"mf_pub_YOUR_PUBLISHABLE_KEY\", { endpoint: \"https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST\", autoPage: true });\n  // when someone signs up or logs in:\n  loomail(\"identify\", \"user_123\", { email: \"user@example.com\" });\n  loomail(\"track\", \"signed_up\", { plan: \"trial\" });\n  // when they log out:\n  // loomail(\"reset\");\n</script>\n```\n\nCalls: `init`, `identify(userId, traits)`, `track(event, properties)`, `page([name], [properties])`\n(sends a `page_viewed` event; `autoPage: true` does one on load, call it yourself on\nroute changes in a single-page app), and `reset()`."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#5",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 2. The browser snippet > Anonymous visitors and remembering people",
    "text": "By default an anonymous visitor is never sent to the server.\nThe snippet keeps their `track`/`page` calls in the browser's localStorage (up to\n50 events, up to 48 hours old) and, the moment you call `identify`, sends them under the\nreal `userId` with their original timestamps. Visitors who never sign up cost nothing and\ncreate no contacts. The snippet also remembers the identified user across page loads, so\n`identify` does not have to run on every page; call `reset()` on logout so the next person\non that browser starts clean.\n\nLimits: this is per browser. A visitor who browses on one device and signs up on another\nis not stitched, and events older than 48 hours are dropped rather than re-dated by the\nserver's clock-skew clamp. If localStorage is blocked, the snippet works for the life of\nthe page only. Every call also carries a stable `context.anonymousId`."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#6",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 2. The browser snippet > Anonymous visitors and remembering people > Server-side anonymous tracking (opt-in)",
    "text": "To lift those limits, turn on `anonymousTracking`:\n\n```js\nloomail(\"init\", \"mf_pub_YOUR_PUBLISHABLE_KEY\", { endpoint: \"https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST\", anonymousTracking: true });\n```\n\nThe snippet then sends `track` and `page` calls from a visitor who has not been identified straight to\nLoomail, keyed by the visitor's `anonymousId`. No contact is created. When you call\n`loomail(\"identify\", userId, traits)`, the identify carries the same `anonymousId`, and everything stored for that\nvisitor moves onto the contact with its original timestamps, with no 48-hour or 50-event limit. After that,\ncalls from the same browser go straight to the contact until `reset()` starts a new anonymous visitor.\n\nYou can do the same from any client with the HTTP API:\n\n```bash\n# before sign-up: a visitor you cannot name yet\ncurl -X POST https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST/v1/track   -H \"Authorization: Bearer mf_live_YOUR_SECRET_KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\"   -d '{\"anonymousId\": \"a1b2c3\", \"event\": \"viewed_pricing\", \"properties\": {\"plan\": \"pro\"}}'\n\n# at sign-up: say who that visitor turned out to be\ncurl -X POST https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST/v1/identify   -H \"Authorization: Bearer mf_live_YOUR_SECRET_KEY\" -H \"Content-Type: application/json\"   -d '{\"userId\": \"user_123\", \"anonymousId\": \"a1b2c3\", \"traits\": {\"email\": \"user@example.com\"}}'\n```"
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#7",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 2. The browser snippet > Anonymous visitors and remembering people > Server-side anonymous tracking (opt-in)",
    "text": "`anonymousId` may be sent at the top level or as `context.anonymousId`. Limits and behaviour:\n\n- At most 100 events are kept per visitor; later ones are dropped until they identify.\n- Events from visitors who never identify are deleted after 30 days. The link from an anonymous id to a contact\n  is kept 90 days.\n- Moved events behave like any other event: they can move the contact's lifecycle state and trigger flows.\n- Merging is by `anonymousId` per workspace and happens on `identify` only. A visitor on two devices has two\n  anonymous ids and is joined when each device identifies.\n- If the same browser identifies as a second person without `reset()`, later events belong to the second person;\n  history already moved stays with the first.\n- This stores data about visitors who never sign up. Turn it on only where your privacy notice and consent setup\n  allow it.\n\nProperties worth knowing:"
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#8",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 2. The browser snippet > Anonymous visitors and remembering people > Server-side anonymous tracking (opt-in)",
    "text": "- **No build step, no dependencies.** Roughly 3 KB gzipped as served.\n- **Load-order independent.** The two-line stub queues calls made before\n  `loomail.js` arrives; the snippet replays them when it loads.\n- **No CORS preflight.** Payloads go out as `text/plain` with the key in the\n  body, which is a CORS-simple request. Nothing triggers an OPTIONS round\n  trip. (`Authorization: Bearer` + `application/json` also works and is what\n  server-side clients use; browsers just pay one preflight for it.)\n- **Survives page unload.** Sends via `navigator.sendBeacon`, falling back\n  to `fetch(..., { keepalive: true })`.\n- **Never breaks your page.** Every code path is wrapped; a down or\n  misconfigured Loomail is invisible to your users.\n- After `identify(userId)`, `track(event, properties)` reuses that user.\n  The explicit form `track(userId, event, properties)` also works."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#9",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 2. The browser snippet > Origin allowlist (optional)",
    "text": "On the Integrate screen, each publishable key can list allowed origins (one\nper line, e.g. `https://app.example.com`). Browsers on other origins get 403\nwith no CORS headers, so the request fails closed. Leave the list empty to\nallow any origin - still safe in the write-only sense; the allowlist is\nabuse friction, not the trust boundary."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#10",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 2b. React, Next.js, Vue and other frameworks",
    "text": "For an app built with a bundler, use the `@loomail/browser` package instead of the script tag. It does what the snippet does (anonymous visitors, `identify`, `reset`, the same storage, a publishable key) and adds:\n\n- **Page views on route changes.** A single-page app never reloads, so `autoPage` in the snippet records only the first page. The package follows the History API, which every client-side router uses (React Router, Next.js, Vue Router, Nuxt, SvelteKit, Angular), and records one page view per new address with the page title set after the route has rendered.\n- **A React provider and hook**, a **Vue plugin**, and a plain `createLoomail()` for everything else. Next.js is supported in the app and pages routers; rendering on the server does nothing.\n- **Consent:** `startOptedOut` and `optIn()` / `optOut()` for cookie banners.\n- **A guard:** a secret key (`mf_live_...`) is refused, with a console error."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#11",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 2b. React, Next.js, Vue and other frameworks",
    "text": "The **Integrate** page has a tab for each framework (HTML, React, Next.js, Vue, Nuxt, SvelteKit, Angular, plain JavaScript, Tag Manager) with your address and key already in the code, and a second row of tabs for sending from your server (Node.js, Python, PHP/Laravel, Ruby/Rails, Go, Java). Each guide was run in a real app against a real Loomail before it was published. For Angular, the CLI's `ng generate environments` step and filling in both environment files matter: `ng serve` uses the development one. The package README (packages/sdk-browser) has the full option and call reference.\n\n```tsx\nimport { LoomailProvider } from \"@loomail/browser/react\";\n\n<LoomailProvider publishableKey={process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_LOOMAIL_KEY!} endpoint=\"https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST\" autoPage\n  user={user ? { id: user.id, traits: { email: user.email } } : null}>\n  {children}\n</LoomailProvider>\n```\n\nPass the signed-in person as `user` and Loomail identifies them; pass `null` and it resets on logout. Use the key in your framework's public environment variables (`NEXT_PUBLIC_`, `VITE_`, `PUBLIC_`, `NUXT_PUBLIC_`), not in the source. A site on a different origin needs that origin in the key's allowlist if you set one (see below)."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#12",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 3. Server-side > Plain HTTP (any language)",
    "text": "Any HTTP client works. Node 18+:\n\n```javascript\nconst LOOMAIL = \"https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST\";\nconst KEY = \"mf_live_YOUR_SECRET_KEY\";\n\nasync function track(userId, event, properties = {}) {\n  const res = await fetch(`${LOOMAIL}/v1/track`, {\n    method: \"POST\",\n    headers: {\n      \"Authorization\": `Bearer ${KEY}`,\n      \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\",\n    },\n    body: JSON.stringify({ userId, event, properties }),\n  });\n  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`loomail track failed: ${res.status}`);\n}\n\nawait track(\"user_123\", \"invoice_paid\", { amount: 4900 });\n```\n\nThe same shape works from Go (`net/http`), Ruby, or anything else that can POST\nJSON with a header. We ship SDKs for Node and Python only; for other languages\nthe endpoint is the SDK. Send a `messageId` with each call so that retrying a\nfailed request cannot double-count it."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#13",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 4. Pointing an existing Segment SDK at Loomail",
    "text": "If you already have Segment instrumentation, you can dual-send or migrate\nwithout touching your event calls.\n\n**@segment/analytics-node** posts batches to `{host}{path}` and\nauthenticates with the write key as the HTTP Basic username, both of which\nLoomail understands:\n\n```javascript\nconst { Analytics } = require(\"@segment/analytics-node\");\n\nconst analytics = new Analytics({\n  writeKey: \"mf_live_YOUR_SECRET_KEY\",\n  host: \"https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST\",  // path defaults to /v1/batch\n  flushAt: 20,                        // batching works; /v1/batch is supported\n  maxRetries: 3,\n});\n\nanalytics.identify({ userId: \"user_123\", traits: { email: \"user@example.com\" } });\nanalytics.track({ userId: \"user_123\", event: \"signed_up\" });\nawait analytics.flush({ close: true });\n```\n\nNotes:\n\n- A `track` item with only an `anonymousId` is accepted as an anonymous visitor's event. An item with neither id,\n  or an `identify` with no `userId`, appears as a per-item error in the batch response, not as a silent drop.\n- `page`/`screen`/`group`/`alias` calls have no endpoint here. Do not set\n  `path` to anything but `/v1/batch`.\n- Payload caps: 100 items and 512 KB per batch request. Segment's own limits\n  (32 KB per call, 500 KB per batch) are tighter than ours, so traffic that\n  Segment accepted fits."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#14",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 4. Pointing an existing Segment SDK at Loomail",
    "text": "Other Segment server SDKs (Python, Go, Java, Ruby) expose the same\nhost/base-URL override and use the same Basic-auth convention; the same two\nsettings port them."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#15",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 5. Google Tag Manager",
    "text": "GTM has no native Loomail destination; use a Custom HTML tag:\n\n1. In GTM: **Tags > New > Custom HTML**.\n2. Paste the browser snippet from section 2 (the whole block, stubs and\n   all), with your publishable key.\n3. Trigger: **All Pages** (plus any event triggers you want `track` calls\n   bound to).\n4. For event-level calls, fire additional Custom HTML tags on your GTM\n   events, e.g. `<script>loomail(\"track\", \"signup_completed\")</script>`.\n\nBecause the snippet queues calls made before `loomail.js` loads, GTM's async\ncontainer loading cannot race it."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#16",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 6. Dual-sending alongside an existing Segment install",
    "text": "Keep Segment exactly as it is and add Loomail as a second sink:\n\n- **Server-side:** instantiate a second `Analytics` client pointed at\n  Loomail (section 4) next to your existing one, and call both. Or add a\n  three-line forwarder in front of your current `analytics.track` call\n  sites using the plain-fetch helper in section 3.\n- **Browser:** load the Loomail snippet (section 2) next to Segment's\n  analytics.js and call both. There is no conflict; the two libraries share\n  nothing.\n- **Via Segment itself:** in the Segment dashboard, add a **Webhook\n  destination** on your source pointing at\n  `https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST/v1/batch` with the secret key as an\n  `Authorization: Bearer` header. Every event Segment receives is forwarded\n  to Loomail. (This is the zero-code option; it depends on Segment's webhook\n  retry behavior for delivery guarantees.)"
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#17",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier)",
    "text": "When a tool can post a webhook but you do not want to write code against `/v1/track`, add a **source** on the\n**Integrate** page (**Connect other tools**). Each source has its own address, `https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST/webhooks/in/<id>`,\nand a log of what it received (kept 30 days). Up to 20 sources per workspace."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#18",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Any JSON webhook",
    "text": "Choose **Any JSON webhook** and say where things are in the payload, using dotted paths such as `data.user.email` or\n`answers.0.text`:"
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#19",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Any JSON webhook",
    "text": "- **The person**: a user id field, an email field, or both. With only an email, the person is matched to an existing\n  contact with that email, or created keyed by it (like the CSV import).\n- **The event**: a fixed name, a name read from a field, or none (the request then only sets traits).\n- **Traits**: any number of `trait name` from `field` pairs (up to 20). Empty or missing fields are skipped, so a request\n  never clears a trait. `company`, `name`, `email`, `plan` and `payment_status` set the built-in fields, as in `identify`.\n- **Payload**: by default the whole JSON body is kept on the event, so flows and segments can use any of it.\n- **The sender's own id for the event** (optional): a path such as `event_id`, or a request header such as `X-Shopify-Event-Id`. If\n  the sender retries a delivery, the same id is recorded once. Senders that can set a header can send `X-Loomail-Event-Id` instead,\n  which wins over both and is used exactly as sent. When the event's name also comes from the request (a path or a header), the name is\n  part of the id, so one booking id on a \"created\" and a \"cancelled\" event are two events, not a repeat.\n- **The event name from a header** (optional): for senders that keep the topic out of the body (Shopify's `X-Shopify-Topic`, WooCommerce's\n  `X-WC-Webhook-Topic`). One source then records every topic, each under its own name."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#20",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Any JSON webhook",
    "text": "**Picking from a list.** A path can step into a list by position (`answers.1.email`) or search it by what an item is\n(`answers[type=email].email`: the `email` of the first item whose `type` is `email`). The key can be dotted\n(`answers[field.ref=work_email].email`), and searches can follow each other. Prefer a search when the list can change length:\na position moves when an optional question is skipped, a search does not. Values are compared as text, so `[number=5]` matches 5.\n\nThe body can be JSON, or form-encoded (`application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, which some tools send by default). A form body is read\nas a flat object of text values; a field sent twice becomes a list. Numbers arrive as text (`\"5\"`), so a trait from a form body is\ntext. Stripe, Paystack and Flutterwave sources need JSON.\n\n**How calls are checked.** Choose when you add the source (it cannot be changed afterwards):"
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#21",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Any JSON webhook",
    "text": "- **A token** (the default), described next.\n- **The sender's own signature.** Some tools sign every call: an HMAC-SHA256 of the exact request body, made with a secret you and the\n  tool share, sent in a header (Shopify `X-Shopify-Hmac-Sha256`, WooCommerce `X-WC-Webhook-Signature`, Tally `Tally-Signature`, Cal.com\n  `X-Cal-Signature-256`). Give Loomail the header name, how the signature is written (hex, base64, or either), any text before it such\n  as `sha256=`, and the shared secret. Loomail stores the secret encrypted, makes no token, and accepts a call only when its signature\n  matches. The address can then be shared with the tool as it is. A new secret can be saved later; the old signature stops working at once.\n  This proves who sent the call, not when: a signed call that is captured and replayed still verifies, and the sender's event id\n  (above) is what keeps a replay from being recorded twice.\n\nThe source gives you a token, shown once. Send it as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`, as `X-Loomail-Token: <token>`, or\nas `?token=<token>` on the address for tools that cannot set headers (the address then is a secret; do not share it).\nAn optional `X-Loomail-Event-Id` header makes a repeat of the same request harmless."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#22",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Any JSON webhook",
    "text": "```bash\ncurl -X POST 'https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST/webhooks/in/SOURCE_ID' \\\n  -H 'Authorization: Bearer mf_in_YOUR_TOKEN' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"event_type\":\"form_response\",\"user\":{\"id\":\"u_1\",\"email\":\"ada@example.com\"}}'\n```\n\nAnswers: `200` when handled, or deliberately ignored (nothing to record); `400` for a body that is not a JSON object (or form) or has no\nperson at the configured paths; `401` for a wrong token, a disabled source or an unknown address (all the same answer, so\nthe address cannot be used to find out which sources exist); `402` when your plan's contact limit stopped a new person;\n`429` past 600 requests a minute for one source."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#23",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Ready-made tools",
    "text": "On the Integrate page, **Connect other tools**, the **Type** list has ready-made settings for the tools below. Pick one, follow the\nsteps it shows, save, and give the tool the address Loomail shows (for tools that cannot send a header, the address includes the\ntoken: keep it private). Each is a generic source with the fields filled in, so you can change any of them afterwards."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#24",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Ready-made tools",
    "text": "| Tool | Becomes | Person | Retries | Checked against the tool's docs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Typeform | `typeform_submitted` | hidden field `user_id`, else the email answer | once, by `event_id` | official example payload |\n| Tally | `tally_form_submitted` | the email question | once, by `eventId` | official page; signature check available; Tally can also send an `Authorization` header |\n| Webflow form | `webflow_form_submitted` | `payload.data.email` (rename to your field) | once, by `payload.id` | field list only: send a test and read the log |\n| Cal.com | the trigger, such as `BOOKING_CREATED` | first attendee's email | once per booking and trigger, by `payload.uid` | official example payload; signature check available |\n| Gumroad | `gumroad_sale` | `email` (form fields, price in US cents) | not deduplicated | Ping field list; no full example found |\n| Shopify | the topic header, such as `customers/create` or `orders/paid` | `email` | once, by `X-Shopify-Event-Id` | topic, event-id and signature headers confirmed; payload fields: check the first delivery |\n| WooCommerce customers | the topic header, such as `customer.created` | `email` | once, by the delivery id header | topic and signature headers confirmed; customer fields: check the first delivery |"
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#25",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Ready-made tools",
    "text": "| Segment | the track call's event name | `userId`, email from `context.traits.email` | once, by `messageId` | official track spec |"
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#26",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Ready-made tools",
    "text": "Where the payload shape is only partly documented, the form says so. Send one real or test delivery and open the source's log: a request\nthe settings cannot read is logged as rejected with the reason, and you can fix the path and send again.\n\nTally, Cal.com, Shopify and WooCommerce sign their calls, and their presets offer the signature check (see \"How calls are checked\"\nabove) as well as a token: you paste the secret that tool signs with. For Shopify and WooCommerce the signature is the better choice,\nbecause neither can send a token header. Webflow, Typeform, Segment and Gumroad: Loomail checks the token only. A WooCommerce order\ncarries its email under `billing`, so order webhooks are logged as rejected: this preset is for customers.\n\nTools without a ready-made preset (Fillout, Formspree, PostHog, Getwaitlist and others) work through **Any JSON webhook** when they can\nPOST JSON or form fields to a URL: send a test, open the log and set the paths from what arrives. Jotform (it wraps the answers in a\nmultipart body) and Google Forms (no webhook) need Zapier or Make in between."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#27",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Typeform",
    "text": "Typeform cannot set headers, so put the token in the address: add the source, then give Typeform\n`https://YOUR_LOOMAIL_HOST/webhooks/in/SOURCE_ID?token=mf_in_YOUR_TOKEN` as the webhook URL (keep it private). Typeform sends\n`form_response` events like this (trimmed):\n\n```json\n{ \"event_id\": \"LtWXD3crgy\", \"event_type\": \"form_response\",\n  \"form_response\": { \"hidden\": { \"user_id\": \"abc123456\" }, \"calculated\": { \"score\": 9 },\n    \"answers\": [ { \"type\": \"email\", \"email\": \"laura@example.com\", \"field\": { \"id\": \"SMEUb7VJz92Q\", \"type\": \"email\" } },\n                 { \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Laura\", \"field\": { \"id\": \"JwWggjAKtOkA\", \"type\": \"short_text\" } } ] } }\n```\n\nSettings that work for it:\n\n| Setting | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Event | named by `event_type`, or always `typeform_submitted` |\n| User id | `form_response.hidden.user_id` (pass your own id to the form as a Typeform hidden field) |\n| Email | `form_response.answers[type=email].email` |\n| Event id | `event_id`, so a retry of the same submission is recorded once |\n| A trait from a short answer | `form_response.answers[field.ref=YOUR_QUESTION_REF].text`, or `[field.type=short_text].text` for the first |"
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#28",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Typeform",
    "text": "Use a search (`[type=email]`) rather than a position (`answers.1.email`): Typeform leaves skipped questions out of `answers`, so every\nlater answer moves up by one. Loomail checks the token in the address; it does not check Typeform's optional `Typeform-Signature`."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#29",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Zapier, Make, n8n and other no-code tools",
    "text": "Use their **Webhooks** (HTTP request) action and set the method to POST. Zapier's *Webhooks by Zapier, POST* can set a header:\nadd `Authorization: Bearer mf_in_YOUR_TOKEN`, or use `?token=` in the URL. Choose **JSON** as the payload type if offered; a form payload\nworks too. Send flat fields (`id`, `email`, `event`, `company`) and point the paths at them. Give each Zap its own source if the\npayloads differ, and send an `X-Loomail-Event-Id` header (or an id field plus the event id path) when a step can run twice."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#30",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Stripe",
    "text": "Choose **Stripe**, then in Stripe (Developers, Webhooks) create an event destination of type **Webhook endpoint** at the address\nshown. Set its payload to **Snapshot**, not Thin: thin events carry only ids, and Loomail needs the details. A thin event is\nacknowledged (so Stripe stops retrying) and noted in the log with this instruction. Select these events, and paste the\nendpoint's signing secret (`whsec_...`) into Loomail. Test mode and live mode have different secrets, so add one source for\neach mode you use.\n\n| Stripe event | What Loomail records |\n|---|---|\n| `checkout.session.completed` | event `checkout_completed`; the Stripe customer id is remembered on the person |\n| `customer.subscription.created` | event `subscription_started`; payment status and plan |\n| `customer.subscription.updated` | event `subscription_updated`; payment status and plan |\n| `customer.subscription.deleted` | event `subscription_cancelled`; payment status `cancelled` |\n| `invoice.paid` | event `payment_succeeded`; payment status `paid` (not for a zero-amount invoice) |\n| `invoice.payment_failed` | event `payment_failed`; payment status `past_due` |"
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#31",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Stripe",
    "text": "Payment status comes from the subscription: `trialing` is `trial`, `active` is `paid`, `past_due` and `unpaid` are `past_due`,\n`canceled` is `cancelled`; an incomplete or paused subscription leaves it alone. The plan is the price's nickname, else its\nlookup key, else its id. Other event types are acknowledged and ignored.\n\n**Matching people.** Loomail uses, in order: your own user id in a Stripe metadata field (the key is configurable, default\n`user_id`; set it on customers, subscriptions or checkout sessions), the checkout's `client_reference_id`, a person who already\ncarries this Stripe customer id, a person with this email, and otherwise creates one keyed by the email. Subscription events carry\nno email, which is why the customer id is remembered the first time Loomail sees it (usually at checkout). Events Loomail cannot\nmatch to anyone are acknowledged and noted in the log, not retried. A person's own email is never overwritten."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#32",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Paystack",
    "text": "Choose **Paystack** on the Integrate page, save, and paste the address it shows into Paystack (Settings, API Keys &\nWebhooks, webhook URL). Paystack has a separate webhook URL for test mode and for live mode, so add one source for each.\n\n**How calls are checked.** Choose one when you add the source (it cannot be changed afterwards):\n\n- **A secret in the address** (the default). Loomail makes a token and puts it in the address you give Paystack\n  (`...?token=mf_in_...`). Loomail holds no Paystack key. Anyone who has the address can send events, so keep it private. You can\n  also turn on **only accept Paystack's published addresses** (52.31.139.75, 52.49.173.169, 52.214.14.220), but only if Loomail sees\n  the real sender address: behind a proxy that hides it, every call would be refused.\n- **Paystack's signature.** Paystack signs each call (`x-paystack-signature`, HMAC-SHA512 of the body) **with your secret key**,\n  so checking it means giving Loomail that key (`sk_test_...` or `sk_live_...`). It is stored encrypted and never shown again, but it\n  is the key that can move money, so choose this only if you are comfortable with that."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#33",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Paystack",
    "text": "| Paystack event | What Loomail records |\n|---|---|\n| `charge.success` | event `payment_succeeded`; payment status `paid` (not for a zero amount); the plan name if the charge is for a plan |\n| `subscription.create` | event `subscription_started`; `paid`, plan |\n| `subscription.not_renew` | event `subscription_not_renewing`; the status is left alone (still paid until the period ends) |\n| `subscription.disable` | event `subscription_cancelled`; status `cancelled` |\n| `invoice.payment_failed` | event `payment_failed`; status `past_due` |\n| `invoice.update` | when paid: event `invoice_paid`; status `paid` |\n\nOther events (refunds, transfers, disputes, `invoice.create`) are acknowledged and ignored. A renewal sends both a\n`charge.success` and an `invoice.update`, so it records both a `payment_succeeded` and an `invoice_paid` event: use whichever suits\nyour flow. Amounts are in the smallest unit (kobo for NGN), as Paystack sends them.\n\n**Matching people.** Your own user id if you pass one in the payment's metadata (key configurable, default `user_id`), otherwise a\nperson who already carries this Paystack customer code, otherwise the customer's email, otherwise a new person keyed by the email.\nPaystack puts the email and customer code on every event Loomail uses."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#34",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Paystack",
    "text": "**Order and repeats.** Paystack events carry no id and no dependable event time, and retries (every 3 minutes four times, then\nhourly for 72 hours) can arrive late. So: an identical redelivery is recorded once and never re-applies the status; a late event that\ncarries its own time (a payment's paid time, an invoice's created time) cannot overwrite a newer payment status; and events with no\ntime of their own (a subscription starting or being disabled) count from when they arrive. A subscription disabled, re-enabled and\ndisabled again is recorded as two cancellations. A one-off purchase also sets `paid`: Paystack does not say whether a charge belongs\nto a subscription."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#35",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Flutterwave",
    "text": "Choose **Flutterwave**, save, and Loomail shows an address and a **secret hash**. In Flutterwave (Settings, Webhooks) paste the address as\nthe URL and the secret hash as the secret hash, and turn on webhook retries. Flutterwave sends the hash back unchanged in the\n`verif-hash` header of every call, and Loomail refuses any call whose header does not match. (Flutterwave offers no payload\nsignature, so this shared secret is the whole check; Loomail makes it random and keeps only its hash.)\n\n| Flutterwave event | What Loomail records |\n|---|---|\n| `charge.completed`, `status: successful` | event `payment_succeeded`; payment status `paid` (not for a zero amount) |\n| `charge.completed`, `status: failed` | event `payment_failed`; the status is **not** changed |\n| `subscription.cancelled` | event `subscription_cancelled`; status `cancelled` |\n\nFlutterwave does not send an event when a subscription starts or renews, and a charge carries nothing that names its plan, so there is\nno plan name and no `past_due` status from Flutterwave. A failed charge is an event only because it could equally be a stranger's\nabandoned checkout or a declined renewal. Other events (transfers, bill payments, refunds) are acknowledged and ignored. Amounts are\nin whole units (100 means 100 NGN), unlike Stripe and Paystack."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#36",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7a. Receiving events from other tools (Stripe, forms, Zapier) > Flutterwave",
    "text": "**Matching people.** Your own user id if you put one in the charge's `meta` (key configurable, default `user_id`), otherwise a person\nwho already carries this Flutterwave customer id, otherwise the customer's email, otherwise a new person keyed by the email. A\ncancellation carries only the email. A charge is recorded once per Flutterwave transaction and status, however many times it is sent\n(Flutterwave may send the same event more than once), and, as with Stripe, a late event cannot overwrite a newer payment status."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#37",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > What the AI sees of the data you send",
    "text": "AI-drafted emails use only a few of the things you send, so it pays to send them in the right fields:\n\n- `identify` with `traits.email`, `traits.name` (the full name), and `traits.company`. These are stored on the contact and shown to the AI. A trait called `first_name` is kept on the contact but the AI does not see it.\n- `traits.plan` is the **name of the plan**, for example `\"Growth\"`. It is stored on the contact and shown to the AI as the contact's plan. The five payment-status words are the exception: `plan: \"paid\"` (or `free`, `trial`, `past_due`, `cancelled`) sets the contact's payment status, as `traits.payment_status` does, and is not kept as a plan name. To set both, send both: `{ \"payment_status\": \"paid\", \"plan\": \"Growth\" }`. Send `plan: null` to remove the plan name (the payment status is left as it is).\n- `traits.payment_status` is only ever a **status**, one of `free`, `trial`, `paid`, `past_due`, `cancelled`. Any other value (a plan name, say) is refused and recorded as a conflict, and the status is left unchanged.\n- Every other trait is stored on the contact, but only used by your own fixed templates.\n- **Event properties are not shown to the AI**, only the event name. Use properties for your own analytics and for fixed templates.\n\nHow to write prompts around this is in FLOW-PROMPTS.md."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#38",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > 7. Verifying your integration",
    "text": "Open **Integrate** in the dashboard. The \"Your first event\" panel polls\nevery few seconds: it shows a waiting state until the first event lands,\nthen flips to the event type, event name, user, and receipt time, plus a\ncount of the last 24 hours. If it does not flip within a few seconds of\nsending, check (in order): the key is not revoked, the origin is allowlisted\n(if you set one), you are within the rate limit, and the host URL is\nreachable from where the event was sent."
  },
  {
    "id": "INGESTION#39",
    "source": "Ingestion guide",
    "heading": "Ingestion: Sending Events to Loomail > Rate limits and error responses",
    "text": "| Limit | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Publishable key | 300 requests/minute |\n| Secret key | 3000 requests/minute |\n| Batch size | 100 items / 512 KB |\n\nOver-limit requests get `429` with a `Retry-After` (seconds) header. The\nlimiter is a fixed one-minute window per key, enforced per API process; in\nthe default single-container community install that is the whole system."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#0",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources",
    "text": "If your customer data already lives in **Postgres**, **MySQL**, **SQL Server**, **Snowflake** or **BigQuery**, Loomail can read it on a schedule and turn each row into traits and events. Flows and segments then work on that data with no code on your side.\n\n**Warehouse sources are switched on by the Loomail team, not automatically.** A warehouse can mean a lot of data, so on **Integrate**, under **Data warehouse**, click **Request access** and tell us which database, roughly how much data, and how often it should refresh. We reply by email and set it up with you. Once it is on, the same card lets you connect a warehouse.\n\nFind it on the **Integrate** page, under **Data warehouse**. Redshift, Supabase, Neon, RDS, Aurora and AlloyDB all speak the Postgres protocol, so use the Postgres connector for them. MariaDB, Aurora MySQL, PlanetScale and TiDB speak the MySQL protocol, and Azure SQL Database speaks SQL Server's, so use those connectors for them.\n\nThis is the opposite of inbound webhooks: there, another system pushes to Loomail; here, Loomail pulls."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#1",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > How it works",
    "text": "1. You write **one SELECT**.\n2. Every N minutes Loomail runs it, asking only for rows at or after where the last run got to.\n3. Each row becomes an `identify` (traits) and, if you named one, a `track` (an event), through the same code as the HTTP API. Plan limits, email-conflict handling and lifecycle rules all apply.\n4. Loomail remembers the highest value of your **position column** and starts there next time.\n\nLoomail wraps your query like this, so you do not write `ORDER BY` or `LIMIT`:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM (<your query>) AS loomail_q ORDER BY <position column> ASC LIMIT <batch size>\n```"
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#2",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > How it works > The one parameter: :cursor",
    "text": "`:cursor` is the saved position, always passed as a bound value (never pasted into the text). It arrives as text, so cast it:\n\n| Warehouse | Example |\n|---|---|\n| Postgres | `WHERE updated_at >= :cursor::timestamptz` |\n| MySQL | `WHERE updated_at >= CAST(:cursor AS DATETIME)` |\n| SQL Server | `WHERE updated_at >= CAST(:cursor AS DATETIME2)` |\n| Snowflake | `WHERE updated_at >= TO_TIMESTAMP_TZ(:cursor)` |\n| BigQuery | `WHERE updated_at >= TIMESTAMP(:cursor)` |\n\nUse `>=`, not `>`. Rows that share the last position value would otherwise be lost. The repeat is harmless: Loomail remembers a fingerprint of the rows at the saved position and skips them when nothing about them changed, and an event's message id is a fingerprint of its row, so a repeat is never recorded twice."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#3",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > How it works > What the query may and may not do",
    "text": "- One statement, starting with `SELECT` or `WITH`. A trailing semicolon is fine.\n- No `$1`, `?` or `@name` parameters, and no dollar-quoted strings. `:cursor` is the only parameter.\n- **SQL Server:** a query that starts with `WITH` (a common table expression) is refused, because SQL Server cannot nest one inside the wrapper Loomail adds for ordering and the row cap. Put the `WITH` part in a view, or write it as a subquery in `FROM`. **MySQL:** `/*! ... */` comments are refused, since MySQL runs their contents as SQL.\n- The **position column** must be in the query's output, named as a plain column (letters, numbers, underscores). Name it with `AS` if needed.\n- Column names in your settings are matched without regard to case. Snowflake upper-cases unquoted names; that is fine.\n\nThis check is a guard rail, not the security boundary. **The boundary is the credential**: connect with a user that can only `SELECT`. Postgres and MySQL sources additionally run inside a `READ ONLY` transaction with a statement time limit. SQL Server has no read-only transaction, so for SQL Server the login is the whole guarantee: give it `db_datareader` and nothing else."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#4",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > Postgres",
    "text": "```sql\nCREATE USER loomail_reader WITH PASSWORD '...';\nGRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE crm TO loomail_reader;\nGRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO loomail_reader;\nGRANT SELECT ON users TO loomail_reader;\n```\n\nGive Loomail the host, port, database, user and password. Encryption defaults to *encrypted, verify the certificate*. Your database must be reachable from wherever Loomail runs; allow its address in your firewall or security group."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#5",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > Postgres > Managed Postgres services",
    "text": "The Postgres connector speaks the ordinary Postgres protocol, so it works with the services built on it. What differs is the address, the certificate and the pooler:"
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#6",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > Postgres > Managed Postgres services",
    "text": "| Service | Host and port | Certificate | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Neon** | `ep-....neon.tech`, 5432. A `-pooler` host is PgBouncer in transaction mode. | Public CA: leave **CA certificate** empty. | Use either host. A scaled-to-zero database takes a few seconds to wake, so the first run after a quiet spell can take longer (it is retried). |\n| **Supabase** | The **session pooler** host from *Connect* (port 5432, user `postgres.<project-ref>`) works from any server. The *direct* host `db.<ref>.supabase.co` is **IPv6 only** unless you buy the IPv4 add-on, and many servers have no IPv6. The transaction pooler (port 6543) also works. | Supabase signs with its own CA: paste the **CA certificate** from *Database Settings* into **CA certificate**, keep encryption on *verify*. | Create a read-only role and use that, not `postgres`. |\n| **Amazon RDS / Aurora** | The instance or cluster endpoint, port 5432. | Amazon's own CA: paste the RDS certificate bundle (the whole file) into **CA certificate**, keep *verify*. | Allow Loomail's outgoing address in the security group. The database must be reachable from the internet, or through a VPN or tunnel you provide. IAM authentication is not supported: use a database user. |"
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#7",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > Postgres > Managed Postgres services",
    "text": "| **Amazon Redshift** (provisioned or Serverless) | The cluster or workgroup endpoint, port **5439**. | Issued by AWS Certificate Manager, publicly trusted: leave **CA certificate** empty. | Redshift must be publicly accessible, with Loomail's address allowed. Use a read-only user. If a query starting with `WITH` is refused, put it in a view and select from the view. |"
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#8",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > Postgres > Managed Postgres services",
    "text": "How the connector behaves with them:\n\n- It opens a read-only transaction (`BEGIN READ ONLY`, supported by Redshift too) and sets a statement timeout for it. If a service refuses the timeout setting, the query still runs, still read-only, and the client's own time limit applies.\n- The certificate is always checked against the host name exactly as you typed it, including when it is an IP address, and the name is sent for SNI (Neon routes on it).\n- Poolers in transaction mode are fine: nothing is kept between transactions.\n- \"Verify\" with a CA you pasted still checks the host name and rejects a certificate that CA did not sign. \"Encrypted, do not verify\" encrypts but accepts any certificate; use it only to get unstuck."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#9",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > Postgres > A database that is not on the internet (SSH tunnel)",
    "text": "This works the same for **Postgres, MySQL and SQL Server**: the examples say `5432`, so use `3306` for MySQL and `1433` for SQL Server.\n\nMost production databases sit on a private network. If you have an SSH server that can reach yours (a bastion or jump host), Loomail can connect to *that* and reach the database through it. Only the SSH server has to be reachable from the internet.\n\n```\nLoomail  ->  your SSH server (public)  ->  your database (private)\n```\n\nIn the form, tick **My database is not reachable from the internet** and fill in:\n\n| Field | What to give |\n|---|---|\n| Database host and port | The database's address **as the SSH server sees it**, such as `db.internal:5432` or `10.0.3.7:5432`. Loomail never resolves or connects to this itself, so a private name is fine. |\n| SSH server, port, user | The bastion's address and the account Loomail signs in as. |\n| Host key fingerprint | `SHA256:...`, so Loomail only talks to your server and nobody can pose as it. Get it from a machine you trust: `ssh-keyscan -t ed25519 bastion.example.com \\| ssh-keygen -lf -` |\n| SSH private key (and passphrase) | The private half of a key pair made for Loomail. Stored encrypted, never shown again. |\n\nSet up the SSH side:\n\n```bash\nssh-keygen -t ed25519 -N \"\" -C \"loomail\" -f loomail_tunnel\n```"
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#10",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > Postgres > A database that is not on the internet (SSH tunnel)",
    "text": "Add the **public** half (`loomail_tunnel.pub`) to the SSH user's `~/.ssh/authorized_keys`, with restrictions in front so that the key can do nothing except open a connection to your database:\n\n```\nrestrict,port-forwarding,permitopen=\"db.internal:5432\" ssh-ed25519 AAAA... loomail\n```\n\nThen paste the contents of `loomail_tunnel` (the private half) into Loomail and delete the local copy.\n\nWhat the SSH server needs:\n\n- `AllowTcpForwarding yes` (or `local`) in `sshd_config`. Some distributions turn it off by default (Alpine does), and when it is off the key cannot override it: you will see *\"it could not reach db.internal:5432\"* even though the address is right. Remember that OpenSSH uses the **first** value it finds.\n- A firewall rule letting Loomail's outgoing address reach the SSH port. A hosted Loomail refuses an SSH server on a private or local address (it must be reachable from the internet); the database behind it may be private.\n- Key authentication. Loomail never offers a password over SSH.\n\nHow it stays safe:"
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#11",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > Postgres > A database that is not on the internet (SSH tunnel)",
    "text": "- The SSH server's host key is **pinned**: a server with any other key is refused, and the message tells you the fingerprint it saw.\n- Each query opens its own tunnel, uses it for exactly one connection on `127.0.0.1`, and closes it afterwards, also when the query fails.\n- The database credential is checked and used exactly as without a tunnel: read-only transaction, time limit, row cap. The database's own TLS certificate is still verified against the host name you typed.\n- Editing a source keeps what you do not retype: changing the password keeps the SSH key, and changing the key keeps the password. Turning the tunnel off deletes the saved key."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#12",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > Postgres > A database that is not on the internet (SSH tunnel)",
    "text": "| Message | Cause |\n|---|---|\n| *The SSH server's host key is not the one you pinned. It is SHA256:...* | The server was rebuilt or replaced, or someone is intercepting the connection. If you expected the change, paste the new fingerprint. |\n| *The SSH server refused the key for user ...* | Wrong user, the public key is not in that user's `authorized_keys`, or the passphrase is wrong. |\n| *The SSH private key could not be read* | Not the whole key (include the BEGIN and END lines), or the passphrase is missing or wrong. |\n| *Connected to the SSH server, but it could not reach db.internal:5432* | The database host or port is wrong as the SSH server sees it, forwarding is disabled in `sshd_config`, or `permitopen` in `authorized_keys` does not list that exact host and port. |\n| *Could not connect to the SSH server* / *did not answer in time* | The SSH server's address or port, or a firewall. These are retried on the next run. |\n| *Could not connect to the database: password authentication failed* | The tunnel is fine; the database user or password is wrong. |\n\nSSH tunnels are for the connectors that dial a host and port: Postgres, MySQL and SQL Server (including RDS and Aurora in a private subnet). Snowflake and BigQuery are cloud services reached over HTTPS and need none.\n\nTunnel notes for MySQL and SQL Server:"
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#13",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > Postgres > A database that is not on the internet (SSH tunnel)",
    "text": "- The database host name you give is used for certificate checking even though the traffic goes through the tunnel, so a private name such as `db.internal` needs a certificate for that name, or the CA pasted in, or *do not verify*.\n- **Azure SQL** may redirect a client to a different gateway address; behind a tunnel that redirect cannot be followed. Use the *Proxy* connection policy for the server, or the *Redirect* policy only without a tunnel.\n- Messages are the same as above; a wrong host or port as the SSH server sees it reads *it could not reach db.internal:3306* (or `:1433`)."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#14",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > MySQL",
    "text": "Works with MySQL 5.7+ and 8.x, MariaDB, Amazon Aurora MySQL, PlanetScale and TiDB.\n\n```sql\nCREATE USER 'loomail_reader'@'%' IDENTIFIED BY '...';\nGRANT SELECT ON crm.users TO 'loomail_reader'@'%';\n```\n\nGive Loomail the host, port (3306), database, user and password. Encryption defaults to *encrypted, verify the certificate*; for Amazon RDS / Aurora paste the RDS certificate bundle as the CA certificate.\n\n- Each run is one prepared statement inside a `START TRANSACTION READ ONLY`, with `max_execution_time` (MySQL) or `max_statement_time` (MariaDB) set to the time limit when the server has them.\n- `DATETIME` columns carry no time zone; Loomail reads them as UTC. Keep the position column in UTC, or convert it in the query (`CONVERT_TZ(updated_at, 'Europe/London', '+00:00') AS updated_at`).\n- `DECIMAL` values arrive as numbers, `JSON` columns as text, and binary columns as base64 text.\n- With the *verify* setting, the server certificate is checked against the host name you typed."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#15",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > SQL Server",
    "text": "Works with SQL Server 2016+ and Azure SQL Database / Managed Instance.\n\n```sql\nCREATE LOGIN loomail_reader WITH PASSWORD = '...';\nUSE crm;\nCREATE USER loomail_reader FOR LOGIN loomail_reader;\nALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER loomail_reader;\n```\n\nGive Loomail the host, **port** (1433 unless you changed it), database, user and password (SQL Server authentication; Windows/Entra sign-in is not supported). Use a port, not an instance name: named instances are found over UDP, which cannot go through a tunnel or most firewalls. For a named instance, find its fixed TCP port in SQL Server Configuration Manager and give that.\n\n- Loomail declares read-only intent and applies the time limit, but SQL Server cannot enforce a read-only transaction. **Use a login that can only read.**\n- Encryption is on by default and the certificate is verified. A self-hosted SQL Server often has a self-signed certificate: either paste its CA as the CA certificate, or choose *encrypted, do not verify*. Azure SQL uses a public CA and needs nothing.\n- Timestamps (`datetime2`, `datetime`) are read as UTC. `decimal`/`money` values arrive as numbers and `uniqueidentifier` as text.\n- Cast `:cursor` explicitly: `CAST(:cursor AS DATETIME2)`."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#16",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > Snowflake",
    "text": "Loomail uses Snowflake's SQL API with **key-pair sign-in** (no password is stored).\n\n```bash\nopenssl genrsa 2048 | openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -inform PEM -out loomail_key.p8 -nocrypt\nopenssl rsa -in loomail_key.p8 -pubout -out loomail_key.pub\n```\n\n```sql\nCREATE ROLE loomail_reader;\nGRANT USAGE ON WAREHOUSE compute_wh TO ROLE loomail_reader;\nGRANT USAGE ON DATABASE analytics TO ROLE loomail_reader;\nGRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA analytics.public TO ROLE loomail_reader;\nGRANT SELECT ON TABLE analytics.public.users TO ROLE loomail_reader;\nCREATE USER loomail_svc DEFAULT_ROLE = loomail_reader RSA_PUBLIC_KEY = '<contents of loomail_key.pub without the BEGIN/END lines>';\nGRANT ROLE loomail_reader TO USER loomail_svc;\n```\n\nIn Loomail enter the **account identifier** (`myorg-myaccount`, or a locator such as `xy12345.eu-west-1`), the user, the compute warehouse and role, and paste the contents of `loomail_key.p8`. If your key is encrypted, add its passphrase. If Snowflake answers 401, the public key on the user does not match the private key you pasted, or the account identifier is wrong. Check with `DESC USER loomail_svc` (the `RSA_PUBLIC_KEY_FP` field).\n\nIf your account uses a network policy, allow your Loomail server's outgoing address."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#17",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Setting up each warehouse > BigQuery",
    "text": "1. Create a **service account** in your Google Cloud project.\n2. Give it **BigQuery Job User** on the project and **BigQuery Data Viewer** on the dataset.\n3. Create a JSON key for it and paste the whole file into Loomail.\n\nSet the **location** if your dataset is not in the default US/EU multi-region (for example `europe-west2`), and the **data limit per query**: every query carries `maximumBytesBilled`, so a mistake fails instead of running up a bill (default 10 GB)."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#18",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > The settings",
    "text": "| Setting | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| Position column / type | The column that rises when a row is added or changed (`updated_at`), and whether it holds a date and time, a number or text. |\n| Start from | Optional first position. Empty reads everything the first time. |\n| Run every | 5 to 1440 minutes. After a failure the next try comes sooner (at most 15 minutes). |\n| SSH tunnel | Optional, Postgres, MySQL and SQL Server: reach a private database through an SSH server. See above. |\n| Rows per batch | 10 to 5000. A run reads up to 5 batches, then carries on next time. |\n| User id / email column | Who the row is about. One is enough; with only an email, people are matched by it. A row with neither is skipped, not an error. |\n| Event | A fixed name, a name read from a column, or none (traits only). |\n| Time column | Optional. Events more than 3 days from the present are recorded at sync time (the same rule as the HTTP API). |\n| Traits | Trait name to column. A `null` value is skipped, never used to clear a trait. |\n| Keep the row's values on the event | The row's non-null columns become the event's properties. |\n\n**Test the query** shows the first rows and what each would become, and records nothing. Use it before saving.\n\n**Sync now** runs a source at once and shows the result. **Read everything again** resets the position; nothing already recorded is doubled."
  },
  {
    "id": "WAREHOUSES#19",
    "source": "Data warehouse guide",
    "heading": "Data warehouse sources > Troubleshooting",
    "text": "| Message | Cause |\n|---|---|\n| *The query does not return these columns: ...* | A column named in the settings is not in the query's output. The message lists what it does return. |\n| *Could not connect to the database* | Host, port, firewall, or password. Postgres errors show the driver's reason. |\n| *Snowflake refused the sign-in (401)* | Public key not set on the user, wrong account identifier, or the wrong key pasted. |\n| *Google refused the service account* | Key file wrong or revoked. |\n| *Query exceeded limit for bytes billed* | The query would read more than the data limit. Narrow the query (filter on a partition column) or raise the limit. |\n| *The query took too long* | A batch has 60 seconds. Make the query faster: filter on an indexed or partitioned column. |\n| *More than N rows share the same ... value* | Raise the batch size. |\n| *Your plan's contact limit is reached* | New people cannot be created until there is room. |"
  },
  {
    "id": "DATA#0",
    "source": "Data export and deletion guide",
    "heading": "Data export and workspace deletion > What erasure removes, and what it keeps",
    "text": "Removed, in one database transaction (all or nothing): the workspace and every row in\nevery table that carries its id: team, sessions, API keys, contacts, events, flows,\nmessages, templates, knowledge base, suppressions, provider credentials, subscriptions,\ncheckout records and the rest. The list of tables is discovered from the database, so a\ntable added later is covered automatically, and a test fails if anything survives.\n\nKept, on purpose:\n\n| What | Why | What it holds |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `deleted_workspaces` | prove that a deletion happened, and when | id, name, slug, plan, dates, how, rows erased per table, and the owner's email only as a SHA-256 hash |\n| `billing_events` | payment records for your accounting | provider event id, type, outcome and the provider payload (which can include the payer's email). Detached from any workspace. |\n| `admin_audit_log` | what operators did | the entries, detached, with the workspace name and slug written in |\n\nIf you must erase payment records too, or you operate where a different retention rule\napplies, decide that with your lawyer and adjust `drizzle/purge/index.ts`.\n\nNot covered: backups of your database (they age out on your backup schedule), emails\nalready sent to people (they hold what was sent), and the payment provider's own records."
  },
  {
    "id": "DATA#1",
    "source": "Data export and deletion guide",
    "heading": "Data export and workspace deletion > Known limits",
    "text": "- Export is JSON only (no CSV, no zip).\n- Owners are emailed when deletion is scheduled, cancelled, or done by an immediate admin\n  erase. They are not emailed when the grace period simply runs out: the scheduling email\n  already gave the date, and the owners are erased along with everything else.\n- Background job queue entries that mention an erased workspace are not removed; they find\n  nothing to act on and finish.\n- The export rate limit is in memory, per process."
  },
  {
    "id": "FAQ#0",
    "source": "FAQ",
    "heading": "FAQ and Troubleshooting > Sending problems > Nothing happens after I send an event",
    "text": "1. **Is there an active, compiled flow whose trigger matches?** All three are required: status `active`, compile status `ready`, and the trigger event name spelled exactly as your app sends it. Event names are case-sensitive strings; `signed_up` and `signed-up` are different events\n2. **Did you send `identify` instead of `track`?** Identify calls never trigger flows. Only `track` events (and lifecycle transitions, and retention-grid segments) enroll contacts\n3. **Event-triggered flows are near-instant, but lifecycle-transition and segment triggers only run on the 15-minute scan.** Wait one scan cycle before concluding it is broken\n4. **Is the contact already in a nurture flow?** One active nurture flow per contact. The new one either evicts it (higher priority) or is blocked\n5. **Re-entry policy?** A `once` flow will not re-enroll a contact who has been through it\n6. **Is the contact suppressed or missing an email address?** Enrollment is blocked for suppressed addresses; messages cannot be sent to contacts with no email\n\nVerify ingestion itself is healthy on the Integrate page (it shows recent event volume) and see INGESTION.md for the verification loop."
  },
  {
    "id": "FAQ#1",
    "source": "FAQ",
    "heading": "FAQ and Troubleshooting > Sending problems > How do I send faster (or slower)?",
    "text": "Settings → Pace, or `PUT /v1/settings/throttle` (owner). Defaults and ranges are tabulated in INSTALLATION.md. The knobs: per-contact daily/weekly caps, minimum gap between emails, send-window hours/days/timezone, and `batch_size_per_tick` (max messages per tenant per drain tick, default 10, range 1-100).\n\nFixed constraints: the drain sweep runs every 15 minutes (approvals and enrollments send within seconds; the sweep is the backstop), and critical-class flows always bypass frequency caps and the window. To go slower per provider, set a `daily_limit` on the transport."
  },
  {
    "id": "FAQ#2",
    "source": "FAQ",
    "heading": "FAQ and Troubleshooting > Sending problems > Why did a contact get no email even though the flow ran?",
    "text": "Look at the message status on the contact or in Sent: `skipped` (the brain's decide step said wait/skip, or a step condition was false), `value_gated` (the assess step judged the draft not worth sending), `suppressed`, `rejected`, or `failed` (with the reason attached). All of these are terminal and deliberate; none of them retry on their own. Generation failures can be retried from the Approvals screen."
  },
  {
    "id": "SENDING#0",
    "source": "Managed sending guide",
    "heading": "Managed sending (Loomail sends your customers' email) > 3. What customers do",
    "text": "*Settings > Email sending > Loomail Sending > Turn on.* They can send at once from the shared\naddress. To use their own domain they enter it (a subdomain such as `mail.theircompany.com` is\nsafest), copy the three DNS records shown into their DNS provider, and press *Check now*. The page\nalso re-checks by itself while the domain is verifying, and starts sending from the domain the moment\nResend says it is verified. If a verified domain later stops verifying (the records were removed),\nthat workspace quietly goes back to the shared address.\n\nWhat is checked when a domain is entered: it must be a real domain name; free email services\n(gmail.com and so on) are refused; your own domains and anything under them are refused; and a domain\ncan belong to only one workspace (a domain exists once in your Resend account). A workspace may\nchange its domain a few times an hour. Changing or removing a domain removes the old one from Resend.\n\nThe test email in *Settings* works through managed sending too."
  }
